									{Fails}
								</p>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>

			<figure class="well mt-16 p-4" data-diagram="context-engine">
				<div class="overflow-x-auto" set:html={ContextEngine} />
				<figcaption class="mt-6">
					<span class="telemetry-tag">M9 // CONTEXT ENGINE</span>
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<h3 id="dispatch" class={`mt-20 ${Head}`}>
				One call, end to end
			</h3>
			<p class={Lede}>
				Two views of one call: what the agent sees, and what the process does. Following one
				call makes the split obvious - every intervention is invisible on the agent's side,
				and every rewrite is decided on the system's side.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-10">
				<ZineFlow
					Title="THE DISPATCH // ONE HOOK CALL"
					Complete="REPLY RETURNED"
					Steps={Dispatch}
				/>
			</div>
			<div class="prose-flow mt-10 max-w-[46rem]">
				<p class={Note}>
					The failure mode of the whole chain sits at the boundary, not in the middle:
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-2 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
					<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
						a hook that raises is logged and skipped
					</li>
					<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
						a hook that returns nothing is a no-op
					</li>
					<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
						and neither ever aborts the turn.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class={Note}>What a hook cannot do is pretend it ran.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- 04 // HOOKS - the six seams as a turn, six cards, the gate that keeps a
		     result raw, and the numbers behind both. -->
		<section class="blueprint-section relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">04 // HOOKS // THE CHAIN</p>
			<h2 id="chain" class={Section}>
				Six hooks, one turn
			</h2>
			<div class="prose-flow mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
				<p class={Body}>
					Every hook is a thin dispatch that forwards its arguments to the Rust dylib and
					hands the JSON reply back to Hermes.
				</p>
				<p class={Body}>
					It is the reason the shim registers one callback per name: on plugin load it
					asks the dylib for its hook names, and the six hooks are always registered
					together.
				</p>
			</div>

			<h3 class={`mt-12 ${Head}`}>The turn, in order</h3>
			<div class="grunge shell-card mt-6" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
				<div class={CardHead}>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--bone">ONE TURN</span>
					<span class="telemetry-tag">session start → next turn</span>
