		<!-- 05 // DOWNLOAD - the install path as a playbook: a session ribbon, the
		     seven lines in a recessed well, a footer that says what the copy control
		     holds, then the catalog route beside it. -->
		<section class="blueprint-section relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">05 // DOWNLOAD // EXPLICIT STEPS ONLY</p>
			<h2 id="download" class={Section}>
				register() never downloads
			</h2>
			<p class={Lede}>
				The split is deliberate: loading a plugin is a read, and fetching a binary is a
				write. Keeping them apart means an agent that starts in a clean environment never
				silently pulls an executable over the network - the fetch is a step a person runs,
				with a verdict attached to it.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
				<div>
					<p class={Body}>Binaries are never shipped in the repository.</p>
					<p class="mt-6 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
						Fetching them is an explicit setup step you run yourself - and the
						verification is mandatory, not best-effort:
					</p>
					<ul class="mt-6 space-y-6 font-body text-body text-bone-newsprint">
						<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
							<span class="font-bold text-bone-white">
								SHA-256 against the in-tree checksum list
							</span>
							-{" "}
							<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
								SHA256SUMS.txt
							</code>
							, generated by CI into the tree; a mismatch is refused outright.
						</li>
						<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
							<span class="font-bold text-bone-white">Magic-byte validation</span>-
							the downloaded file must be a real ELF / Mach-O / PE image, or it is
							rejected.
						</li>
						<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
							<span class="font-bold text-bone-white">Backup + restore</span> - an
							existing install is backed up and restored if any step fails. No
							release-hosted fallback.
						</li>
						<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
							<span class="font-bold text-bone-white">
								A missing binary disables, not breaks
							</span>
							- the plugin logs the setup command and stays disabled until the
							binaries are present.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p class="mt-8 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						The four checks are ordered by cost, not by importance:
					</p>
					<ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
							the checksum is one hash
						</li>
						<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
							the magic bytes are four
						</li>
						<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">the backup is a rename</li>
						<li class="border-l-2 border-oxblood-dark pl-3">
							and the last one is not a check at all - it is what the plugin does when
							every check before it has already failed.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<!-- THE PLAYBOOK. One shell card: a session ribbon, the seven lines in
				     a recessed well, and a footer callout - the shape a reader follows
				     with a terminal open beside it. -->
				<div class="grunge shell-card self-start" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
					<div class={CardHead}>
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--teal">SESSION // BASH</span>
						<span class="telemetry-tag">ROUTE 01 // GIT CLONE</span>
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--canary">
							{InstallSteps} STEPS
						</span>
						<span class="telemetry-tag ml-auto">READY</span>
					</div>
					<!-- `bg-soot-black` is carried for the CODE PLATES rule in Global.css:
					     the fence drops its own rhythm only inside a soot inset or an
					     asphalt panel, and this host is one. The fill that renders is the
					     well's, because Global.css loads after the utility layer - the
					     class is the selector here, not the colour. -->
					<div class="well bg-soot-black p-2 sm:p-3">
						<CodeFence Code={InstallFence} CopyPayload={InstallCommands} Copy />
					</div>
					<div class="flex flex-col gap-2 border-t border-[color:var(--tier-shell-edge)] px-5 py-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between">
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--canary">
							COPY THE FULL SEQUENCE:
						</span>
						<span class="font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">
							the COPY tab on the fence copies all seven lines
						</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p class="mt-8 max-w-[46rem] font-mono text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
				The copy payload is {InstallSteps} lines: clone, link, fetch, enable, start. The{" "}
				<span class="text-weathered-teal">ln -s</span> line is the one that makes the repo
				the plugin Hermes sees - the install directory is a symlink, not a copy.
			</p>

			<!-- ROUTE 02 - the same install, from the catalog. -->
			<div class="grunge shell-card mt-12" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
				<div class={CardHead}>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--bone">
						ROUTE 02 // PLUGIN CATALOG
					</span>
					<span class="telemetry-tag">reviewed · pinned commit</span>
				</div>
				<div class={CardBody}>
					<p class={Body}>
						ALSO ON THE STORE: the plugin is in the Hermes plugin catalog - the
						reviewed, pinned alternative to the manual clone.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							hermes plugins install aphrodite
						</code>
						checks out the catalog entry's exact commit, then
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							bash download.sh
						</code>
						from{" "}
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							~/.hermes/plugins/aphrodite
						</code>
						still fetches the binaries (never
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">aphrodite setup</code>
						against the catalog-managed directory).
					</p>
					<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						The full flow is on
						<a
							class="text-weathered-teal underline transition-colors hover:text-bone-white"
							href={Routes.Setup}
						>
							SETUP
						</a>
						; the mechanics are in{" "}
						<a
							class="text-weathered-teal underline transition-colors hover:text-bone-white"
							href={ExternalLinks.HermesGuide}
						>
							the Hermes plugin guide
						</a>
						.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3 id="key" class={`mt-16 ${Head}`}>
				The proxy needs its own key
			</h3>
			<div class="prose-flow mt-6 max-w-[46rem] border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-5">
				<p class={Note}>
					LLM API KEY REQUIRED: the proxy is an OpenAI-compatible forwarder and needs its
					own credentials -{" "}
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">APHRODITE_API_KEY</code>,
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">aphrodite setup</code>, or
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">api_key</code> in
					~/.hermes/aphrodite/aphrodite.toml.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					Hermes' provider config is NOT reused, and there is no keyless mode: without a
					key the proxy refuses to start.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					That refusal is the point. A forwarder that started without a key would accept a
					request, hold it, and fail it somewhere downstream - so the proxy checks first
					and exits with the missing variable named.
				</p>
			</div>

			<h3 id="config" class={`mt-16 ${Head}`}>
				What happens without a config file
			</h3>
			<div class="prose-flow mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
				<p class={Note}>
					A MISSING CONFIG IS REPORTED, NOT INFERRED: when
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">aphrodite.toml</code> is
					absent the plugin says so, and the binary falls back to a single token-mode
					listener on the first port - the second proxy never binds, and every
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">[compression]</code> knob,
					the store retention caps included, is simply absent.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					It is the reason the install runs
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">aphrodite setup</code>:
					the config file is written before the binary is ever started, so the fallback
					never becomes the everyday state.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					The fallback is a floor, not a mode: one listener, token compression, no
					retention promise. It exists so a fresh checkout can answer a request before
					anyone has written a config, and it is reported every time it is in effect.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-10">
				<ZineLesson
					Label="FAILS SAFE"
					Color="raw"
					Lines={[
						"Every step degrades in the same direction:",
						"a missing binary disables the plugin",
						"a missing key stops the proxy",
						"a missing config narrows it to one listener.",
						"None of them runs half-configured and calls it success.",
					]}
				/>
			</div>

			<ZineSource
				Lines={[
					"SRC: plugins/aphrodite/download.sh · SHA256SUMS.txt (generated by CI)",
					"SRC: docs/guides/hermes-integration.md:115-141",
				]}
			/>
		</section>

		<!-- 06 // FILESYSTEM - the loader surface: a shell card holding the tree in
		     an inset well, then the three files that matter, then the self-heal. -->
		<section class="blueprint-section relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">06 // FILESYSTEM // CANONICAL LAYOUT</p>
			<h2 id="layout" class={Section}>
				What changes after install
			</h2>
			<p class={Lede}>
				Two directories, two owners. Hermes owns the plugin directory and the plugin owns
				everything under the runtime home - the arrow annotations are the whole map of who
				writes what.
			</p>

			<div class="grunge shell-card mt-8" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
				<div class={CardHead}>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--bone">FILESYSTEM TOPOLOGY</span>
					<span class="telemetry-tag">~/.hermes/</span>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--teal">2 owners</span>
				</div>
				<div class="well m-4 p-5 font-mono text-code leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
					<div>~/.hermes/</div>
					<div class="pl-4">
						├── plugins/aphrodite{" "}
						<span class="text-zinc-dust">← manual symlink to the plugin repo</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-4 text-bone-white">
						└── aphrodite/ <span class="text-zinc-dust">← runtime home</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-10">
						├── binaries/aphrodite{" "}
						<span class="text-zinc-dust">← proxy binary, fetched + verified</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-10">├── binaries/libaphrodite_hermes.dylib</div>
					<div class="pl-10">
						├── aphrodite.toml <span class="text-zinc-dust">← config</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-10">
						├── directives/{" "}
						<span class="text-zinc-dust">← 7 built-ins materialized</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-10">
						├── ccr.db <span class="text-zinc-dust">← SQLite CCR store</span>
					</div>
					<div class="pl-10">
						└── proxy-stderr.log{" "}
						<span class="text-zinc-dust">← proxy logs (empty = healthy)</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
				<div class="grunge shell-card" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
					<div class={CardHead}>
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--crimson">binaries/</span>
					</div>
					<div class={CardBody}>
						<p class={Body}>
							The only two files that execute. Both arrive through
							<span class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
								download.sh
							</span>{" "}
							and both are verified before they land; a failed fetch restores the pair
							that was already there.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="grunge shell-card" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
					<div class={CardHead}>
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--crimson">directives/</span>
					</div>
					<div class={CardBody}>
						<p class={Body}>
							The seven built-ins, materialized on first start so an edited directive
							is never silently overwritten by an upgrade. A deleted one comes back; a
							changed one stays changed.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="grunge shell-card" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
					<div class={CardHead}>
						<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--crimson">ccr.db</span>
					</div>
					<div class={CardBody}>
						<p class={Body}>
							The address space the markers point into. Delete it and the markers stop
							resolving - the previews in the transcript still read, which is why the
							store can be dropped without losing the conversation.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3 id="selfheal" class={`mt-16 ${Head}`}>
				The self-heal, and where it stops
			</h3>
			<div class="prose-flow mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
				<p class={Note}>
					The runtime home self-heals toward
					<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
						layout_schema.json
					</code>{" "}
					at startup: the self-heal moves misplaced config and binaries to their canonical
					paths and quarantines stale plugin-source copies.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					It is the reason the self-healing stops at the Hermes-owned boundary: the
					~/.hermes/plugins/aphrodite install dir is Hermes' territory, and the plugin
					never creates, converts, or modifies it.
				</p>
				<p class={Note}>
					The schema is the authority, not the directory listing: a file is misplaced when
					it is not where the schema says, and the repair is a move - never a copy, never
					a rewrite. Anything it cannot place it quarantines instead of deleting, so a
					stale copy is set aside with its bytes intact and the next start does not trip
					over it again.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-8">
				<ZineReveal
					Cover="QUARANTINE // stale plugin-source copy, moved aside with its bytes intact"
					Tag="MOVED ASIDE"
					Raw="<span class='font-mono text-base text-stamped-canary'>~/.hermes/aphrodite/quarantine/&lt;timestamp&gt;/plugins-aphrodite</span>"
				/>
			</div>

			<figure class="well mx-auto mt-16 max-w-md p-3" data-diagram="hook-chain-mini">
				<div class="overflow-x-auto [&_svg]:h-auto" set:html={HookChain} />
				<figcaption class="mt-4">
					<span class="telemetry-tag">M3 MINI // HOOK CHAIN</span>
				</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<ZineSource Lines={["SRC: plugins/aphrodite/layout_schema.json"]} />
		</section>

		<!-- 07 // EVOLUTION - the lanes, the pairing rule, and the count that was
		     corrected. -->
		<section class="blueprint-section relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<p class="section-index">07 // EVOLUTION // HOW THE SURFACE GREW</p>
			<h2 id="evolution" class={Section}>
				The six hooks, the version pairing, and the 14-to-13 correction
			</h2>
			<p class={Lede}>Three things grew at once:</p>
			<ul class="mt-6 max-w-[46rem] space-y-2 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
				<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">the seams</li>
				<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
					the pairing rule that keeps a plugin pointing at a binary that exists
				</li>
				<li class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-4">
					and the discipline that keeps the declared tool count honest.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-6 max-w-[46rem] font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
				Read together they explain why the plugin can be installed from a tag and verified
				against a checksum list.
			</p>

			<h3 id="lanes" class={`mt-12 ${Head}`}>
				Six lanes, one trigger each
			</h3>
			<div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3">
				{Lanes.map(({ Hook, When, Does }) => (
					<div class="grunge shell-card" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
						<div class={CardHead}>
							<h4 class="font-mono text-code font-bold text-bone-white">{Hook}</h4>
						</div>
						<div class={CardBody}>
							<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--canary">{When}</span>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								{Does}
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
			<p class="mt-8 max-w-[46rem] font-mono text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
				SRC: docs/plugin/hooks.md:13-20 - every hook is a thin dispatch that forwards to the
				Rust dylib; the dylib holds all the behavior.
			</p>

			<div class="grunge shell-card mt-16" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
				<div class:list={["h-0.5 w-full", Accent.raw]} />
				<div class={CardHead}>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--crimson">
						THE VERSION PAIRING · BINARY_VERSION MECHANICS
					</span>
				</div>
				<div class={CardBody}>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 lg:grid-cols-3">
						<div>
							<div class="font-headline text-sub font-black text-bone-white">
								{Stamps.Pairing()}
							</div>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								Today's pairing: binary {Versions.Binary}, plugin {Versions.Plugin}.
							</p>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								Every release moves both: "
								{Versions.ChangelogPairing.replaceAll("`", "")}"
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<div class="font-headline text-sub font-black text-bone-white">
								BUMPED LAST
							</div>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
									BINARY_VERSION
								</code>{" "}
								is pinned to a binary release tag the plugin can download.
							</p>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								Since the first plugin-first release (v1.4.6), the plugin is tagged
								<em>before the parent</em>, so the pinned version points at assets
								that already exist.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<div class="font-headline text-sub font-black text-bone-white">
								OR IT REFUSES
							</div>
							<p class="mt-3 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
								The plugin warns before{" "}
								<code class="font-mono text-base">download.sh</code> when the pinned{" "}
								<code class="font-mono text-base">BINARY_VERSION</code> points at a
								release with no assets - and since v1.6.5, a checksum mismatch is a
								hard refusal, not a warning.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-8 max-w-[46rem] font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						The order is the mechanic. Tagging the plugin first means the pinned version
						can only ever point backwards, at a release whose assets are already
						uploaded and hashed - so the download has something to verify against before
						anyone asks for it.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-6 font-mono text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						SRC: CHANGELOG.md v1.4.6/v1.4.4/v1.6.5/v1.6.6 entries · the Aphrodite/v1.4.6
						release note ("released plugin-first ... points at assets that already
						exist")
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="grunge shell-card mt-12" data-grunge data-grunge-mode="edge">
				<div class:list={["h-0.5 w-full", Accent.canary]} />
				<div class={CardHead}>
					<span class="telemetry-tag telemetry-tag--canary">
						13 TOOLS, NOT 14 · THE CORRECTION IS A BEAT
					</span>
				</div>
				<div class={CardBody}>
					<p class={Body}>
						v1.6.5 corrected the manifest: "14 tools" → "13 tools" - the release dylib
						registers exactly the 13 declared tools.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
						The 14th,{" "}
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">aphrodite_debug</code>
						, is{" "}
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							debug_assertions
						</code>
						-gated out of release builds, matching what{" "}
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							hermes plugins validate
						</code>
						enforces.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
						Earlier, v1.6.1 had already removed it from
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">provides_tools</code>,
						closing the catalog-review mismatch on the pinned tree - and v1.4.6 had done
						the same for{" "}
						<code class="font-mono text-base text-weathered-teal">
							aphrodite_navigate
						</code>
						, the navigation tool whose feature was unbuildable.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
						The declared surface and the running surface are kept identical on purpose.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 font-body text-body leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						The count is not cosmetic: the validator compares the manifest's tool list
						against the schemas the dylib reports, so a release that advertises fourteen
						while registering thirteen fails that comparison before a user ever runs it.
						A debug-only tool is therefore a build artifact, not a declared surface -
						and the manifest says so.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 font-mono text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-dust">
						SRC: CHANGELOG.md:61-64 (v1.6.5) · CHANGELOG.md:166-173 (v1.6.1) · v1.4.6
						release note
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<ZineSource
				Lines={[
					"SRC: CHANGELOG.md v1.6.5 · CHANGELOG.md v1.6.1 · the Aphrodite/v1.4.6 release note",
					"SRC: plugins/aphrodite/plugin.yaml (provides_tools)",
				]}
			/>
		</section>

